---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="length-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="length" from="cm" to="in" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a length</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type your number into either field. The other side updates on each keystroke, so you can work in either direction.</li>
      <li>Pick the units. The list has groups for metric, imperial and US, and nautical and astronomical units, so millimetres are easy to find.</li>
      <li>If you picked the pair the wrong way round, use the swap button. It keeps the equation and does not reset it.</li>
      <li>Set the decimal places for a fixed number of digits. For drawings and cut lists, three decimals of a millimetre is usually enough.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Each pair in the "common conversions" list has its own link, so you can bookmark
      <code>?from=cm&amp;to=in</code> and open the conversion you use most.
    </p>

    <h2>Which factors are exact and which are not</h2>
    <p>
      Most common length conversions are exact. Since the 1959 international yard and pound agreement,
      the inch is exactly 25.4 mm, so the foot is exactly 0.3048 m, the yard exactly 0.9144 m and the
      mile exactly 1,609.344 m. Those conversions have no rounding. The 2.54 in a formula is the
      definition, not a measurement.
    </p>
    <p>
      The nautical mile is also exact, at 1,852 m, because it was redefined from "one minute of
      latitude" to a round metric number. The astronomical unit was fixed at exactly
      149,597,870,700 m in 2012. The light-year follows from it, because the speed of light is a
      defined constant. The only rounding comes from the decimal places you choose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note: the US survey foot, used in older American land records, is two parts per million longer
      than the international foot. It was officially retired at the end of 2022. Over a city block the
      difference is invisible. Over a state boundary it is metres, so surveyors care. This converter
      uses the international foot throughout.
    </p>

    <h2>Reference: metric prefixes on the metre</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Unit</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Metres</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Kilometre</td><td>km</td><td>1,000</td><td>Road distances, running races</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Metre</td><td>m</td><td>1</td><td>Rooms, fabric, height</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Centimetre</td><td>cm</td><td>0.01</td><td>Body measurements, paper sizes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Millimetre</td><td>mm</td><td>0.001</td><td>Engineering drawings, timber, screws</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Micrometre</td><td>µm</td><td>0.000001</td><td>Paint thickness, chip features, dust</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Nanometre</td><td>nm</td><td>0.000000001</td><td>Wavelengths of light, semiconductors</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick mental conversions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>cm to inches: divide by 2.5 and take a little off. 30 cm is just under 12 in.</li>
      <li>metres to feet: multiply by 3 and add 10 %. 10 m becomes 33 ft, against 32.8 exact.</li>
      <li>km to miles: multiply by 6 and drop a zero, so 8 km is about 4.8 mi (true value 4.97).</li>
      <li>miles to km: use the Fibonacci trick. Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are about a mile/km pair, so 5 mi ≈ 8 km and 8 mi ≈ 13 km.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are estimates. The length converter above uses full double precision and rounds only when
      it shows the result, so you copy the most accurate value at the precision you asked for.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
